From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] aic_load_fw: match only the vendor interface, and decline with -ENODEV

Two defects, one symptom. On the delivery image every boot logs

  aic_load_fw 2-1.1:1.0: probe with driver aic_load_fw failed with error -1
  aic_load_fw 2-1.1:1.1: probe with driver aic_load_fw failed with error -1
  aic_load_fw 2-1.1:1.2: probe with driver aic_load_fw failed with error -1

at dev_err level, right where the WiFi/BT subsystem comes up -- and yet WiFi
and Bluetooth both work. It reads as a failed radio and is not one.

Why three lines. aicwf_usb_id_table matched with USB_DEVICE(), which sets only
USB_DEVICE_ID_MATCH_DEVICE (include/linux/usb.h:1015), i.e. idVendor+idProduct
and nothing about the interface. USB binds per interface, so usb_probe_interface()
calls the loader's probe once for every interface of a matching device. After the
firmware upload the part re-enumerates as a69c:8d81 with bNumInterfaces=3 --
two Bluetooth interfaces at class/subclass/protocol e0/01/01 for btusb, and one
vendor-specific ff/ff/ff for aic8800_fdrv -- so the loader is asked three times
and refuses three times.

Why a shout instead of a decline. The refusal returned a bare -1. That is -EPERM
(include/uapi/asm-generic/errno-base.h:5), and drivers/base/dd.c:643-660 gives
-ENODEV and -ENXIO their own arm -- logged as dev_dbg "rejects match" -- while
everything else falls into the default arm and is logged as dev_err. So a
deliberate "not mine" was spelled as a driver failure.

The fix, and why it is the driver's own rule rather than a new claim about the
hardware: aic_load_fw only ever drives the vendor-specific interface. Its own
aicwf_parse_usb() enforces that, rejecting anything that is not ff/ff/ff with
"non WLAN interface" and -ENODEV, for every part id without exception. Matching
the whole device can therefore never gain the loader an interface it would keep;
it can only cost probe calls it must refuse. The table is narrowed to
USB_DEVICE_AND_INTERFACE_INFO(..., 0xff, 0xff, 0xff) -- exactly what the vendor's
own aic8800_fdrv already does for 8801/8d81/8d41/8800DC/8d81x2 -- and the one
remaining legitimate refusal (vendor interface of a loaded part, fw_loaded == 1)
returns -ENODEV.

What this deliberately does NOT change: the fw_loaded == 0 path, where the module
is loaded while the part is already at its post-download id and rwnx_send_reboot()
has to push it back into download mode. That path runs on the vendor interface,
which still matches.

Measured on the board, 2026-08-15, delivery image:
  - 2-1.1 is a69c:8d81, bDeviceClass ef, bNumInterfaces 3
  - 2-1.1:1.0 e0/01/01 -> btusb, 2-1.1:1.1 e0/01/01 -> btusb,
    2-1.1:1.2 ff/ff/ff -> aic8800_fdrv
  - the loader's own probe print names the id it was called with:
    "aicwf_usb_probe vid:0xA69C pid:0x8D81 icl:0x0 isc:0x0 ipr:0x0", three times,
    icl/isc/ipr all zero because USB_DEVICE() leaves those match fields unset.

Not a leak and not a race: the refusal returns before the first kzalloc, and
usb_probe_interface()'s err path (drivers/usb/core/driver.c) already rebalances
intfdata, LPM and runtime-PM and leaves the interface UNBOUND for the next driver
-- which is how btusb and aic8800_fdrv bind it today. This patch removes noise
and a mis-declared match; it does not change what ends up bound.

